---
<LegalLayout title="Privacy — Kuchi" description={description} toc={toc} jsonLd={legalPage('WebPage', '/privacy', 'Kuchi privacy policy', description)}>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <h1>Kuchi privacy</h1>
    <p class="legal-meta">Last updated: <time datetime={LEGAL_UPDATED}>{LEGAL_UPDATED_LABEL}</time></p>
    <p>Kuchi is Kalaris Labs’ collection of launch-video references: real product-launch videos, measured frame by frame, that you can watch on this website and that AI agents use as templates through an open MCP server. This policy explains, plainly and completely, what data Kuchi handles, why, for how long, who helps us, and the rights you have.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="summary">In short</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Kuchi has no accounts, no sign-in and no uploads. We never ask for your name or email address to use it.</li>
    <li>Website analytics uses no cookies and stores nothing in your browser. Your IP address is not saved with any analytics event; we keep only the country reported by Cloudflare.</li>
    <li>Do Not Track and Global Privacy Control switch website analytics off completely, and you can switch it off yourself on the <a href="/analytics-preferences">analytics preferences</a> page.</li>
    <li>Product details your agent sends to plan or check a remake are used to answer that one request. They are never stored and never sent to analytics.</li>
    <li>We do not sell or share personal information, show advertising, or build profiles of people.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="who-we-are">Who we are</h2>
  <p>Kuchi is operated by Kalaris Labs, an independent AI research lab based in India (<a href="https://kalarislabs.com">kalarislabs.com</a>). For the data described here, Kalaris Labs is the data fiduciary under India’s Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 (DPDP Act) and the controller under the EU and UK General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR). Sayan Chowdhury, the founder of Kalaris Labs, is responsible for this policy. You can reach us about anything in it at <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. Operator details are on the <a href="/legal">legal notice</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="scope">What this policy covers</h2>
  <p>This policy covers the Kuchi website at <code>kuchi.kalarislabs.com</code>, the MCP server at <code>https://kuchi.kalarislabs.com/mcp</code>, the agent files under <code>/agent/</code>, the component registry under <code>/r/</code>, the video and image files served from <code>kuchi-media.kalarislabs.com</code>, and the status page at <code>status.kalarislabs.com</code>. The Kalaris Labs website at kalarislabs.com has its <a href="https://kalarislabs.com/privacy">own privacy policy</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="no-accounts">No accounts</h2>
  <p>Kuchi has no accounts and no sign-in. You can browse the collection, play videos and connect an agent to the MCP server without giving us your name, email address or any other account information. Kuchi does not accept uploads.</p>

  <h2 id="your-agent">Your agent and service providers</h2>
  <p>Agents connect to Kuchi’s MCP server without credentials. Its tools return reference videos, their measurements and remake plans. When your agent asks Kuchi to plan or check a remake, it sends details of your product (its name, description, features, on-screen copy and a description of your assets). Kuchi uses them to answer that one request and does not store them or send them to analytics. Your agent provider handles your conversations under its own privacy policy; Kuchi does not receive them.</p>
  <p>Cloudflare hosts the website, the videos and the MCP service. It processes request information, including IP addresses, to deliver the service and prevent abuse (requests are rate-limited per IP address; for this we keep only a count of recent requests, deleted after about an hour without requests). Operational logs may include request times, paths and errors.</p>

  <h2 id="categories">Data we process</h2>
  <p>This is every category of data Kuchi handles. Most of it does not identify anyone; where it could, we say so.</p>
  <div class="legal-table"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">What it contains</th><th scope="col">Where it comes from</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Request data</td><td>IP address, user agent, requested address, time, and the country Cloudflare derives from the connection</td><td>Every request to the website, the media files and the MCP server</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rate-limit counts</td><td>How many requests came from one IP address (or, for IPv6, one /64 network) in the last minute and hour. No request contents</td><td>Requests to the MCP server and the agent files</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Operational logs</td><td>Request times, paths, status codes and errors, for a sample of about one request in ten</td><td>The website and MCP Workers</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Website analytics events</td><td>Described in <a href="#website-analytics">Website analytics</a></td><td>Pages of this website, unless analytics is off</td></tr>
      <tr><td>MCP analytics events</td><td>Described in <a href="#mcp-analytics">MCP server analytics</a></td><td>Agents calling the MCP server and fetching agent files</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Product details for a remake</td><td>Product name, description, features, on-screen copy, a description of your assets, and your agent’s shot analysis</td><td>Your agent, when it calls <code>kuchi_suggest_templates</code>, <code>kuchi_plan_remake</code> or <code>kuchi_check_remake</code>. Used for that one answer, never stored</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Feedback notes</td><td>The note an agent writes with the feedback tool, <code>kuchi_send_feedback</code></td><td>Your agent, only when it sends feedback</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Email</td><td>Your email address and whatever you choose to write</td><td>You, if you email us</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Browser storage</td><td>Your theme, likes and saves, banner and analytics choices. Never sent to us</td><td>Your own browser, described in <a href="#browser-storage">Cookies and browser storage</a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Kuchi does not collect sensitive personal data such as health, financial, biometric or precise location data, and it does not collect government identifiers.</p>

  <h2 id="website-analytics">Website analytics</h2>
  <p>We use PostHog, hosted in the United States, to understand how Kuchi is used. Analytics requests go through this site’s own <code>/relay</code> path.</p>
  <p><strong>On the website</strong> we record page views, clicks on buttons and links, reference viewing, filter use (how long a search is and how many results it gave, never the words), connection attempts, page performance and script errors. Nothing is stored in your browser for this: no cookies and no local storage, so each visit is counted on its own and we cannot recognize you when you return. We do not record sessions or what you type. Your IP address is used to deliver the request and is not saved with the event; we keep only the country reported by Cloudflare. Analytics are switched off completely when your browser sends Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control.</p>
  <p>In more detail, an event can carry: the page address and the site that linked to it; the text, type and position of the element clicked and where on the page clicks land (heatmaps); browser, operating system, device type, screen size and language, as read from your browser; page-speed measurements (Core Web Vitals); the message and location of a script error; on the home page, the campaign tags (<code>utm_source</code>, <code>utm_medium</code>, <code>utm_campaign</code>) in the address you arrived at; and a random identifier created for that page load and forgotten when you leave it. The relay forwards no cookies and no IP address to PostHog, and tells PostHog not to derive a location from one. Custom events can only carry the fields on a fixed list, so search text, prompts, email addresses and full links cannot reach analytics by accident.</p>
  <p><strong>Your switch.</strong> You can turn website analytics off, or back on, for your browser on the <a href="/analytics-preferences">analytics preferences</a> page. When it is off, no analytics request leaves your browser.</p>

  <h2 id="mcp-analytics">MCP server analytics</h2>
  <p><strong>On the MCP server</strong> we record which tool ran, how long it took, whether it failed, the name and version of the connecting client, its country, the catalog search terms and filters used, which reference was requested, and a one-line description of the task if the agent chooses to send one. We do not record your product details, the plans Kuchi returns, or IP addresses. If an agent uses the feedback tool, we store the note it writes.</p>
  <p>The server keeps no sessions. So that one connection’s calls can be counted together, the first message of a connection is given a random session token holding only a random number and the client’s name and version; the agent sends it back with later calls. It identifies a connection, not a person. We also count, without any personal data, when a client is first rate-limited, when someone uses the “connect to Claude” redirect, and which agent file was fetched. A client that makes more than 300 requests in an hour is still answered but no longer counted. Kuchi’s own health checks are never counted.</p>
  <p>None of this is linked to a name, an email address or an account, because Kuchi has none.</p>

  <h2 id="browser-storage">Cookies and browser storage</h2>
  <p>Kuchi sets no cookies. A few conveniences are remembered in your own browser’s storage. They stay on your device, are never sent to us or to analytics, and you can clear them at any time in your browser settings.</p>
  <div class="legal-table"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Name</th><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">What it remembers</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>kuchi-theme</code></td><td>Local storage</td><td>Light or dark mode, if you pick one</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>kuchi-favorites</code></td><td>Local storage</td><td>The videos and components you like or save</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>kuchi-launch-week</code></td><td>Local storage</td><td>That you closed the launch banner</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>kuchi-analytics</code></td><td>Local storage</td><td>That you switched website analytics off on the analytics preferences page. Strictly necessary for that choice to work</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>kuchi-splash</code></td><td>Session storage</td><td>That the logo intro already played in this tab. Cleared when you close the tab</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Cloudflare, which protects the site, may set a strictly necessary security cookie when it needs to tell people from automated traffic. It is not used for analytics or advertising.</p>

  <h2 id="purposes">Purposes and legal bases</h2>
  <div class="legal-table"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Purpose</th><th scope="col">Data used</th><th scope="col">Legal basis</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Delivering the website, videos and MCP tools you ask for</td><td>Request data; product details for the one request they arrive with</td><td>Performing the service you request (GDPR Art. 6(1)(b)); under the DPDP Act, data you provide voluntarily for that purpose (section 7(a))</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Keeping Kuchi secure and available, and preventing abuse</td><td>Request data, rate-limit counts, operational logs</td><td>Legitimate interests in running a safe, reliable free service (GDPR Art. 6(1)(f)); under the DPDP Act, the same specified purpose of providing the service you use</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Understanding how Kuchi is used, to improve it</td><td>Website and MCP analytics events</td><td>Legitimate interests (GDPR Art. 6(1)(f)), with the data kept so that it does not identify you, and an off switch you control. Your browser’s Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control signal is always respected</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fixing what agents report</td><td>Feedback notes</td><td>Legitimate interests (GDPR Art. 6(1)(f)); provided voluntarily for that purpose (DPDP Act section 7(a))</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Answering your email and requests</td><td>Email</td><td>Your request; provided voluntarily for that purpose (DPDP Act section 7(a))</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Meeting legal obligations</td><td>Only what the law requires</td><td>Legal obligation (GDPR Art. 6(1)(c)); compliance with law (DPDP Act section 7)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>We use data only for these purposes. We do not use it for advertising, for profiling, or to train AI models.</p>

  <h2 id="retention">How long we keep data</h2>
  <div class="legal-table"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">How long</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Product details for a remake</td><td>Not stored. Discarded once the answer is sent</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rate-limit counts</td><td>Deleted after about an hour without requests</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Operational logs</td><td>A few days, under Cloudflare’s log retention (at most 7 days), then deleted</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Website and MCP analytics events, feedback notes</td><td>Kept in PostHog for as long as they help us understand and improve Kuchi. They carry no name, email address, account or IP address</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Email</td><td>As long as needed to handle your message and any follow-up, then deleted</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Browser storage</td><td>On your device until you clear it; session storage until you close the tab</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>The catalog, storyboard images and agent files Kuchi caches at the edge contain no personal data. If a law requires us to keep a record for longer, we keep it only for the period and the purpose that law sets, and then delete it.</p>

  <h2 id="processors">Service providers</h2>
  <p>These are all the services that handle data for Kuchi. Each processes it on our behalf and under its own data-protection terms. We do not sell personal information or share it with anyone else.</p>
  <div class="legal-table"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Provider</th><th scope="col">What it does for Kuchi</th><th scope="col">Data it handles</th><th scope="col">Where</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Cloudflare, Inc.</td><td>Hosting and delivery: Workers run the website and the MCP server; Durable Objects hold the rate-limit counts; R2 stores the videos, posters and storyboard images; the edge cache keeps copies of public files; Workers Logs hold the sampled operational logs</td><td>Request data, rate-limit counts, operational logs</td><td>Cloudflare’s global network, usually the data centre nearest you</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PostHog, Inc.</td><td>Product analytics for the website and the MCP server, and storage of feedback notes</td><td>Analytics events and feedback notes, without IP addresses</td><td>United States</td></tr>
      <tr><td>GitHub, Inc.</td><td>Hosts the public repository the MCP server reads Kuchi’s catalog from. Kuchi’s server fetches it; no visitor data is sent</td><td>None about you</td><td>United States</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Our email provider</td><td>Receives and stores mail sent to {CONTACT_EMAIL}</td><td>Email</td><td>Under the provider’s terms</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>The website’s fonts, scripts and styles are served from Kuchi itself; no third-party font service or script host is contacted when you load a page. Links to source posts and to agent apps take you to other services, which have their own privacy policies.</p>

  <h2 id="transfers">International transfers</h2>
  <p>Kalaris Labs is based in India, Cloudflare processes requests across its global network, and PostHog stores analytics in the United States. If you are in the EU, the UK or Switzerland, transfers to these countries rely on the safeguards our providers offer, such as standard contractual clauses. The DPDP Act allows transfers outside India except to countries the Government of India restricts by notification; we will follow any such restriction.</p>

  <h2 id="security">Security and breach notification</h2>
  <p>Kuchi is built to hold as little as possible: no accounts, no passwords, no stored product details. All traffic uses HTTPS, the relay strips cookies and IP addresses before anything reaches PostHog, and request bodies to the MCP server are size-limited.</p>
  <p>If a personal data breach happens, we will tell the people affected without delay, in plain language, with what happened, the likely consequences and what we are doing about it. Under the DPDP Rules, 2025 we will also tell the Data Protection Board of India without delay and send it a full report within 72 hours; where the GDPR applies we will tell the competent supervisory authority within 72 hours unless the breach is unlikely to put anyone at risk.</p>

  <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
  <p>Kuchi is intended for users aged 13 and older, and it is made for people creating product launch videos. Because there are no accounts, we do not knowingly collect personal data from children. We never track or monitor the behaviour of children, or of anyone, and we never show targeted advertising. Under the DPDP Act a child is anyone under 18; if you are under 18, use Kuchi with a parent or guardian’s permission. If you believe a child has sent us personal data, for example by email, write to us and we will delete it.</p>

  <h2 id="ai-agents">AI agents and automated decisions</h2>
  <p>Kuchi’s tools are designed for AI agents. They return measurements and plans computed from the catalog; they do not hold conversations, and they make no decisions about people. Kuchi does not use personal data to train AI models. The agent you use, and the company that provides it, decide what your agent sends to Kuchi and what it does with the answers, under their own terms. Calls an agent makes are not tied to a person: Kuchi never learns who is behind an agent.</p>

  <h2 id="us-privacy">California and other US states</h2>
  <p>We do not sell personal information, and we do not share it for cross-context behavioural advertising, as the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA, as amended by the CPRA) defines those terms. The same holds under the privacy laws of other US states: no sale, no targeted advertising and no profiling that produces legal or similarly significant effects. Kuchi collects no sensitive personal information. We treat Global Privacy Control as a valid opt-out request, and it turns website analytics off. In the last 12 months, the categories of personal information Kuchi handled were internet activity (request data and analytics events), approximate location (country) and, if you emailed us, identifiers (your email address), for the purposes listed above, and disclosed only to the service providers listed above. California residents have the rights listed below, and we will never treat you differently for using them.</p>

  <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
  <p>Depending on where you live, you have some or all of these rights. We offer them to everyone.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Access:</strong> a summary of the personal data we hold about you, what we do with it, and who we have shared it with.</li>
    <li><strong>Correction:</strong> correction, completion or updating of inaccurate or incomplete data.</li>
    <li><strong>Erasure:</strong> deletion of your personal data, unless the law requires us to keep it.</li>
    <li><strong>Withdraw consent and object:</strong> stop processing based on your consent or our legitimate interests at any time, as easily as you gave it. For website analytics, the <a href="/analytics-preferences">analytics switch</a> does this at once.</li>
    <li><strong>Restriction and portability:</strong> under the GDPR, limit how we use your data or receive it in a portable format.</li>
    <li><strong>Know, delete, correct and opt out:</strong> under the CCPA and other US state laws, including the right to opt out of sale and sharing (which we never do) and the right not to be discriminated against for using your rights.</li>
    <li><strong>Grievance redressal:</strong> a response from us to any complaint about how we handle your data, described in <a href="#grievance">Grievance officer and complaints</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Nomination:</strong> under the DPDP Act, name another person to exercise your rights if you die or become unable to.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="exercise-rights">How to exercise your rights</h2>
  <p>Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=Privacy`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with “Privacy” in the subject line and tell us what you would like. You can also ask someone to act for you (an authorised agent or, under the DPDP Act, your nominee); we may ask them to show they have your permission.</p>
  <p>Because Kuchi keeps no accounts and no analytics data tied to a person, we often hold nothing that can be linked to you. If so, we will tell you, and we will not ask for more information than we need to answer. We reply within 30 days. Using your rights is free.</p>

  <h2 id="grievance">Grievance officer and complaints</h2>
  <p>Our grievance officer is Sayan Chowdhury, founder of Kalaris Labs. Write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=Grievance`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with “Grievance” in the subject. We acknowledge grievances promptly and resolve them within 30 days, well inside the 90 days the DPDP Rules, 2025 allow.</p>
  <p>If you are not satisfied with our answer, you can complain to the Data Protection Board of India once our grievance process has run its course; in the EU or the UK, to your local data protection authority (in the UK, the Information Commissioner’s Office); and in California, to the California Privacy Protection Agency or the Attorney General.</p>

  <h2 id="changes">Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>When Kuchi changes in a way that affects your data, we update this page before the change takes effect and change the date at the top. The page always shows the current version.</p>

  <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions or removal requests: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.</p>
